    "internalWorking": "(1) Mark-Sweep: Phase 1 traces roots and sets mark bits in header. Phase 2 walks the entire memory block and adds unmarked blocks to a Free List. Problem: Memory fragmentation\u2014allocating large arrays fails even if total free space is sufficient. (2) Mark-Sweep-Compact: Phase 1 marks live objects. Phase 2 calculates relocation addresses and slides all live objects to one end of the heap space in address order. Phase 3 updates all referencing pointers. Result: Clean contiguous free space for bump-the-pointer allocations, but requires high STW time to rewrite all memory pointers. (3) Mark-Copy: Splits memory into dual semi-spaces. Live objects are traversed and copied contiguously to the destination space, while old space is wiped clean in a single pointer reset.",
    "complexPart": "Pointer Relocation Overhead in Mark-Compact: Compaction requires 3 passes over the heap: Pass 1 calculates the new destination address for every live object; Pass 2 updates every pointer in every object in the heap to point to its new destination; Pass 3 physically copies the byte payload to the new location. Because pointer update cost is proportional to the TOTAL heap size and reference density, Full GC STW pauses scale linearly with heap size on older collectors.",
